# E-OS code owners — required reviewers for the matched paths.
# Docs: https://docs.github.com/articles/about-code-owners
#
# ── READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING THIS FILE ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every owner below is the same handle. That is not a placeholder, it is finding
# **C-18**: this project has one maintainer, one account, one set of credentials
# (docs/security/github-configuration.md §13). A routing table with one destination
# cannot route. GitHub does not let the author of a pull request approve it, so
# `require_code_owner_review: true` here would demand an approval from the person who
# wrote the change — it makes every PR unmergeable except by an admin bypassing the
# rule, which is worse than no rule. §3.3 of that document therefore sets
# `required_approving_review_count: 0` and `require_code_owner_review: false`, and
# `scripts/setup-github-security.sh` derives the second from the first — one line,
# `[ "$APPROVALS" -ge 1 ] && codeowners="true"`. The day a second maintainer exists,
# `--approvals 2` turns this file on in the same command; nothing here has to change.
#
# Second reason it is currently inert: gitlab.com/e-os/e-os is the source of truth and
# github.com/Gh0s777tt/E-OS is a push-mirror of every branch (ADR-0001;
# docs/security/github-configuration.md §0). Code arrives at GitHub because GitLab
# pushed it, already merged — not through a pull request, which is the only event that
# fires CODEOWNERS. And Code Owner *approval* on GitLab is Premium-only, so the mirror
# is not a way around that either.
#
# So what is this file for, today?
#   1. It is the machine-readable answer to "who is on the hook for this path". GitHub
#      uses it to suggest reviewers on any PR opened here, with or without a rule.
#   2. It is the list that becomes real the moment C-18 is closed. Writing it now means
#      the exit criterion is "add a person", not "add a person AND work out the paths".
#   3. It marks the blast radius: everything below can sign, boot, fetch, publish or
#      gate. A diff touching one of these paths deserves a second look even when the
#      only available second look is the author's own, a day later.
#
# What is deliberately NOT here: per-path owners that do not exist, and any claim that a
# match enforces review. Do not invent a handle to make a section look plural.
#
# Mechanics: GitHub applies the LAST matching pattern, so the default sits first and
# specificity increases downward. Since every line resolves to the same handle, order
# changes nothing today — which is itself a fair summary of the state of this file.

# ── Default ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
